Consider the classical maximum-entropy (MaxEnt) inference framework based on the Shannon–Jaynes Principle of Indifference: given a probability distribution p over a measurable state space, one assigns p by maximizing the Shannon entropy S[p] = -∫ p(x) log p(x) dx (or, relative to a prior q, minimizes the Kullback–Leibler cross-entropy D(p‖q) = ∫ p(x) log(p(x)/q(x)) dx) subject to a finite set of expectation-value constraints. This construction yields exponential-family distributions and, in physical applications, leads to standard Fickian/Fourier diffusion equations and additive (Euler homogeneous of order one) thermodynamic potentials.

There are, however, nonequilibrium phenomena — including anomalous (Lévy-type) diffusion, fractal Brownian motion, and self-organized criticality — whose stationary or transient statistics are governed by heavy-tailed, non-exponential distributions for which additivity of the entropy fails and the standard Fick/Fourier diffusion equations are inadequate.

Problem. Formulate precise mathematical criteria (on the underlying dynamics, the constraint algebra, or the geometry of the admissible probability simplex) characterizing exactly those situations in which the Shannon–Jaynes Principle of Indifference fails to produce the physically correct probability assignment. In each such regime, identify a generalized entropy functional S_g[p] and an associated cross-entropy (relative information) functional D_g(p‖q) that:

(i) reduce to the Shannon and Kullback–Leibler functionals in an appropriate limit;
(ii) when maximized/minimized under physically natural constraints, yield the observed non-exponential (e.g. Lévy-stable, q-exponential, stretched exponential, or power-law) distributions;
(iii) generate evolution equations consistent with anomalous diffusion, fractal kinetics, and scale-invariant critical statistics;
(iv) satisfy a coherent set of information-theoretic axioms (positivity, concavity, a suitably weakened additivity/composability rule, and a data-processing inequality).

Determine whether a single such generalized entropy suffices universally, or whether a family of inequivalent entropies (parameterized by the physical context) is necessary, and characterize this family.

